      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "From (2) and (3), we see that both p(xt|yt) and p(xt|yt) can",
      "bbox": [
        312.0000305175781,
        142.36944580078125,
        563.100830078125,
        162.4242401123047
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "be obtained recursively. Once p(xt|yt) has been computed, it",
      "bbox": [
        311.99951171875,
        154.36944580078125,
        563.0491943359375,
        174.4242401123047
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "is possible to compute the expected value E[g(xt)|yt] w.r.t.",
      "bbox": [
        311.9996643066406,
        166.2491455078125,
        563.1305541992188,
        186.30393981933594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "p(xt|yt) for any function g(xt) of interest. A particular case",
      "bbox": [
        311.99981689453125,
        178.2491455078125,
        563.0232543945312,
        198.30393981933594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of Eq. (1) is given by",
      "bbox": [
        312.0001220703125,
        190.1292724609375,
        401.3413391113281,
        203.41943359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u001a xt+1",
      "bbox": [
        383.16033935546875,
        203.22802734375,
        414.368408203125,
        240.17945861816406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "=",
      "bbox": [
        424.8001708984375,
        211.14794921875,
        432.5411376953125,
        221.110595703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Atxt + wt",
      "bbox": [
        442.56036376953125,
        211.14794921875,
        485.0458068847656,
        222.55084228515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "yt",
      "bbox": [
        406.56036376953125,
        223.14801025390625,
        414.48602294921875,
        233.55264282226562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "=",
      "bbox": [
        424.8001708984375,
        223.14801025390625,
        432.5411376953125,
        233.11065673828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Ctxt + vt,",
      "bbox": [
        442.56036376953125,
        223.14801025390625,
        485.6399230957031,
        234.5509033203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(4)",
      "bbox": [
        551.400146484375,
        214.249267578125,
        563.1177368164062,
        227.5394287109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "with wt ∼N(0, Qt), vt ∼N(0, Rt), x0 ∼N(ˆx0, P0), and",
      "bbox": [
        312.0001525878906,
        238.6094970703125,
        563.1009521484375,
        260.1041259765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where the matrices At, Ct, Qt, Rt are assumed to be known",
      "bbox": [
        311.9996337890625,
        250.6094970703125,
        563.2213134765625,
        265.3394775390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "at each time step t. Then the conditional PDF p(xt|yt) is also",
      "bbox": [
        311.9996337890625,
        262.4896240234375,
        562.9810791015625,
        282.54443359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Gaussian N(ˆxt, Pt) where ˆxt = Atˆxt−1 +Lt[yt −CtAtˆxt−1],",
      "bbox": [
        312.0,
        274.4896240234375,
        563.0111083984375,
        295.9842529296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Pt = AtPt−1AT",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        288.407958984375,
        383.66094970703125,
        300.9107666015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "t + Qt −LtStLT",
      "bbox": [
        378.9605712890625,
        288.407958984375,
        456.7405700683594,
        307.9842529296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "t , St = Ct[AtPt−1AT",
      "bbox": [
        452.0401916503906,
        286.4896240234375,
        550.4608764648438,
        302.1798095703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "t +",
      "bbox": [
        545.760498046875,
        289.50830078125,
        562.9811401367188,
        301.8709716796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Qt]CT",
      "bbox": [
        312.00018310546875,
        300.2880859375,
        338.66070556640625,
        311.7926330566406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "t + Rt, Lt = [AtPt−1AT",
      "bbox": [
        333.2400207519531,
        298.36981201171875,
        443.78070068359375,
        313.87103271484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "t + Qt]CT",
      "bbox": [
        439.080322265625,
        300.2882080078125,
        485.3005676269531,
        313.87109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "t S−1",
      "bbox": [
        479.8802795410156,
        299.4302062988281,
        503.8882141113281,
        313.87115478515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "t",
      "bbox": [
        493.08026123046875,
        306.40814208984375,
        496.08599853515625,
        312.8728942871094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "and where T",
      "bbox": [
        508.56036376953125,
        298.36993408203125,
        561.6586303710938,
        311.66009521484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "denotes the transpose operator. These are the equations of the",
      "bbox": [
        312.0004577636719,
        310.36993408203125,
        563.0238647460938,
        323.66009521484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Kalman ﬁlter.",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        322.36993408203125,
        367.7708435058594,
        335.66009521484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "III. COHERENT LOWER PREVISIONS",
      "bbox": [
        360.96063232421875,
        349.1297302246094,
        513.9508056640625,
        362.4198913574219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In this section we give an overview of the theory of coherent",
      "bbox": [
        321.96063232421875,
        365.2098388671875,
        563.2908935546875,
        378.5
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "lower previsions. This is a theory of probability generalized",
      "bbox": [
        312.0004577636719,
        377.2098388671875,
        563.2218017578125,
        390.5
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to handle imprecisely speciﬁed probabilities through sets of",
      "bbox": [
        312.00048828125,
        389.0899353027344,
        563.1181030273438,
        402.3800964355469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "distributions. Despite being a theory of probability, its for-",
      "bbox": [
        312.00048828125,
        401.0899353027344,
        563.1180419921875,
        414.3800964355469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "mulation may look unusual to the reader familiar with more",
      "bbox": [
        312.00048828125,
        412.97003173828125,
        563.1439208984375,
        426.26019287109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "traditional ways to present probability, and this can make the",
      "bbox": [
        312.00048828125,
        424.97003173828125,
        563.02392578125,
        438.26019287109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "theory somewhat uneasy to access. Because of this fact, we",
      "bbox": [
        312.00048828125,
        436.97003173828125,
        563.0234375,
        450.26019287109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "shall point out here informally some of the differences in the",
      "bbox": [
        312.00048828125,
        448.8497314453125,
        563.0238647460938,
        462.139892578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "formulations, in order to help the reader have a smoother start",
      "bbox": [
        312.0004577636719,
        460.8497314453125,
        563.0490112304688,
        474.139892578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "into the theory. In particular, in this section we shall review the",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        472.8497314453125,
        563.02392578125,
        486.139892578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "main concepts of CLPs that we shall use later in the paper to",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        484.7298278808594,
        562.9815673828125,
        498.0199890136719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "derive the solution of the ﬁltering problem. We refer the reader",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        496.7298278808594,
        563.2390747070312,
        510.0199890136719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to [21] for an in-depth study of coherent lower previsions, and",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        508.72979736328125,
        563.102783203125,
        522.0199584960938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to [25] for a survey of the theory.",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        520.6099243164062,
        451.0513610839844,
        533.9000854492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Probability theory is most often deﬁned, after Kolmogorov,",
      "bbox": [
        321.9606018066406,
        532.7297973632812,
        563.4913330078125,
        546.0199584960938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "using a triple made of a sample space, a sigma algebra, and a",
      "bbox": [
        312.00042724609375,
        544.7297973632812,
        563.0234985351562,
        558.0199584960938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "probability function P. The functions from the sample space",
      "bbox": [
        311.99993896484375,
        556.6099243164062,
        563.1431884765625,
        569.9000854492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "into the real numbers that are measurable with respect to the",
      "bbox": [
        311.99993896484375,
        568.6099243164062,
        563.0234985351562,
        581.9000854492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sigma algebra are called random variables. The expectation of",
      "bbox": [
        311.99993896484375,
        580.6099243164062,
        563.1173095703125,
        593.9000854492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "a random variable is deﬁned on the basis of the probability P.",
      "bbox": [
        311.99993896484375,
        592.4900512695312,
        563.0106201171875,
        605.7802124023438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Conditional probability is also deﬁned using P but only when",
      "bbox": [
        311.99993896484375,
        604.4900512695312,
        563.1016235351562,
        617.7802124023438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the conditioning event is assigned positive probability by P.",
      "bbox": [
        311.99993896484375,
        616.4900512695312,
        559.6505737304688,
        629.7802124023438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The theory of CLPs has its focus on expectation rather than",
      "bbox": [
        321.95989990234375,
        628.6099243164062,
        563.101318359375,
        641.9000854492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "probability. We still have the sample space (which is usually",
      "bbox": [
        311.9997253417969,
        640.4900512695312,
        563.1000366210938,
        653.7802124023438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "referred to as the possibility space Ω). We also have a set of",
      "bbox": [
        311.99969482421875,
        652.4900512695312,
        563.1174926757812,
        665.7802124023438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "random variables, which are called gambles: these are bounded",
      "bbox": [
        311.99981689453125,
        664.3696899414062,
        563.3411865234375,
        677.6598510742188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "functions from the possibility space to the real numbers. The",
      "bbox": [
        311.9990234375,
        676.3696899414062,
        563.1423950195312,
        689.6598510742188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "set of gambles does not need to be concerned with measur-",
      "bbox": [
        311.9989929199219,
        688.3696899414062,
        563.1167602539062,
        701.6598510742188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ability questions, that is, it can be chosen arbitrarily. Finally,",
      "bbox": [
        311.9989929199219,
        700.2498168945312,
        563.1295166015625,
        713.5399780273438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "a CLP is deﬁned as a functional, from the set of gambles",
      "bbox": [
        311.99896240234375,
        712.2498168945312,
        563.3143920898438,
        725.5399780273438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to the real numbers, that satisﬁes some rationality criteria",
      "bbox": [
        311.99896240234375,
        724.2498168945312,
        563.1423950195312,
        737.5399780273438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(self-consistency). This function is conjugate to another that",
      "bbox": [
        311.9989929199219,
        736.1299438476562,
        563.0475463867188,
        749.4201049804688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    }
  ],
